-- title:
--   The transition function of the chain generated by a Q-matrix is sub-Markovian: $\sum_y P_t(x,y)\le 1$
-- statement:
--   Let $E\subseteq\mathbb Z$ and let $Q$ be a (not necessarily conservative) Q-matrix on $E$: $Q(x,y)\ge0$ for $x\neq y$ in $E$ and $\sum_{y\in E,\,y\neq x}Q(x,y)\le Q(x):=-Q(x,x)<\infty$. Let $(X,\mathbb P)$ be the continuous-time Markov chain built from $Q$ by the jump chain/holding time construction (from $x$ wait an $\mathrm{Exp}(Q(x))$ time, then jump to $y\ne x$ with probability $Q(x,y)/Q(x)$, the missing mass going to the cemetery), with transition function $P_t(x,y)=\mathbb P_x(X_t=y)$. Then for every $t\in\mathbb R$ and every $x$,
--   $$\sum_{y\in\mathbb Z}P_t(x,y)\le 1 .$$
--
--   That is, $P_t(x,\cdot)$ is a sub-probability measure: $\sum_y P_t(x,y)=\mathbb P_x(t<\zeta)$. In particular every $P_t(x,y)$ is finite, which is what allows the $[0,\infty]$-valued transition function to be manipulated as a real number in generating-function arguments (e.g. Lemmas 5.2–5.3).
--
--   **Formalization Note** `transition E Q t x y` is the platform's path-sum encoding of $P_t(x,y)$ (sum over jump-chain paths of the path weight times the probability, under i.i.d. $\mathrm{Exp}(1)$ clocks, that exactly $n$ jumps have occurred by time $t$); it is $0$ off $E\times E$. The sum is over all integers $y$.
-- source:
--   Loeffen, Patie, Wang, Fluctuation Theory of Continuous-Time, Skip-Free Downward Markov Chains with Applications to Branching Processes with Immigration, arXiv:2208.14425v1, §2, pp. 2–3: the chain constructed from Q by the jump chain/holding time items (i)–(iv), with transition function P_t(x, y) = ℙ_x(X_t = y)

import Mathlib
